# ===========================================================================
# dids.csv — the pool of DIDs this SBC asserts as caller ID.
#
#     cp dids.csv.example dids.csv && chmod 600 dids.csv && $EDITOR dids.csv
#
# dids.csv is gitignored. Every number below is in the 555-01XX range that
# NANPA reserves for fiction and testing. NONE of these are real, and none of
# them will render — render.sh will load them fine, but a call asserting
# 555-01XX will be rejected by any carrier. Replace them with the DIDs you
# actually own on the FracTEL subaccount.
#
# FORMAT
#
#   npa,did
#
#   npa   3-digit destination area code this DID is used for, or the literal
#         OVERFLOW for the fallback pool. Case-insensitive.
#   did   10 or 11 digits. Punctuation is stripped and the value is normalised
#         to 11 digits (a leading 1 is added if absent), so 212-555-0101,
#         2125550101 and 12125550101 are all the same number.
#
# Blank lines and lines beginning with # are ignored. A "npa,did" header row
# is optional and ignored if present.
#
# SELECTION
#
#   1. Destination NPA has a pool  -> rotate within that pool
#   2. It does not, or every DID in it is at its daily cap
#                                  -> rotate within OVERFLOW, log WARN
#   3. OVERFLOW is capped out too  -> refuse with 503, log ERROR
#
# There is no single default number anywhere in that path. That is the whole
# point: a silent fallback onto one DID is what put 50,864 calls on a single
# number in an afternoon and tripped the carrier's fraud alert.
#
# RULES render.sh ENFORCES
#
#   - every did is 11 digits after normalisation, with NPA and NXX both
#     starting 2-9. A malformed row is a hard error with its line number, not
#     a silently skipped line.
#   - the OVERFLOW pool holds at least OVERFLOW_MIN numbers (default 20).
#   - duplicates within a pool are collapsed, and a DID appearing in more than
#     one pool is reported: its daily cap is shared across every pool it is
#     in, because the counter is keyed on the number.
#
# SIZING
#
# A pool needs enough numbers that DID_DAILY_CAP x pool_size comfortably
# exceeds the daily volume you expect into that NPA. At the default cap of 200
# a four-number pool carries 800 calls/day into that area code before it
# starts overflowing. Watch did_selection_reason=overflow in the CDR — a
# rising overflow rate for one NPA means that pool is undersized, not that
# anything is broken.
# ===========================================================================

npa,did

# --- New York -------------------------------------------------------------
212,2125550101
212,2125550102
212,2125550103
212,2125550104

# --- Los Angeles ----------------------------------------------------------
310,3105550101
310,3105550102
310,3105550103
310,3105550104

# --- San Francisco --------------------------------------------------------
415,4155550101
415,4155550102
415,4155550103
415,4155550104

# --- Chicago --------------------------------------------------------------
773,7735550101
773,7735550102
773,7735550103
773,7735550104

# ===========================================================================
# OVERFLOW — used when the destination NPA has no pool of its own, or when
# every DID in that pool has hit its daily cap.
#
# At least OVERFLOW_MIN (20) numbers. render.sh refuses to render with fewer.
# This pool absorbs every area code you have not bought numbers in, so it
# takes more traffic than any single NPA pool and obeys the same daily cap.
# Twenty is a floor, not a target — size it against your actual overflow rate.
# ===========================================================================
OVERFLOW,6465550101
OVERFLOW,6465550102
OVERFLOW,6465550103
OVERFLOW,6465550104
OVERFLOW,6465550105
OVERFLOW,6465550106
OVERFLOW,6465550107
OVERFLOW,6465550108
OVERFLOW,6465550109
OVERFLOW,6465550110
OVERFLOW,6465550111
OVERFLOW,6465550112
OVERFLOW,6465550113
OVERFLOW,6465550114
OVERFLOW,6465550115
OVERFLOW,6465550116
OVERFLOW,6465550117
OVERFLOW,6465550118
OVERFLOW,6465550119
OVERFLOW,6465550120
OVERFLOW,6465550121
OVERFLOW,6465550122
OVERFLOW,6465550123
OVERFLOW,6465550124
